# Will a timber floor joist pass its deflection check over this span?
#
# The serviceability limit is span/300, and the span is itself uncertain -- so
# the limit is uncertain too. A constraint between two ranges is exactly the
# sort of thing a spreadsheet cannot express honestly.
#
#   plimsoll examples/timber-joist.plim --svg joist.svg

given span      = 4.6 .. 4.8 [m]
given udl       = 1.5 .. 2.5 [kN/m^2]    # dead plus imposed, domestic floor
given spacing   = 0.4 .. 0.6 [m]         # joist centres
given width     = 0.047 .. 0.05 [m]      # sawn 47 mm, allowing for tolerance
given depth     = 0.20 .. 0.25 [m]
given e_modulus = 8 .. 11 [GPa]          # C24 softwood, along the grain

line_load = udl * spacing

# Second moment of area of a rectangle. The exponent has to be an exact integer
# for the dimensions to work out, and it is: m * m^3 = m^4.
second_moment = width * depth^3 / 12

# Midspan deflection of a simply supported beam under uniform load.
deflection = 5 * line_load * span^4 / (384 * e_modulus * second_moment)

# Serviceability limit. Both sides are ranges.
deflection <= span / 300

plot depth, deflection
